@page "/blogs/advanced-dotnet-garbage-collection-measure-before-you-tune"
@using BaseComponents
@inherits BasePage

<Content FileName=@nameof(AdvancedDotNetGarbageCollection)>
    <ContentBody>
        <What>
            <p>
                The .NET garbage collector can clean up objects your program no longer uses, but your application may still
                appear to use a lot of memory. That is because cleaning up an object, returning memory to Windows or Linux, and
                closing a file or network connection are different things.
            </p>

            <p>
                The <Highlight>garbage collector (GC)</Highlight> is the .NET runtime feature that manages memory for objects
                created by your C# code. It follows references from places your running program can access, such as local
                variables and static fields. It can reuse memory when no reachable code can get to an object, but it does not
                promise to return that memory to the operating system straight away.
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
 STACK (current method)        MANAGED HEAP
┌──────────────────────┐      ┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ order ───────────────┼─────&gt;│ Order ─────&gt; Item[]          │ reachable
│ count = 3            │      │                              │
└──────────────────────┘      │ "old draft"  (no references) │ unreachable
 static field ───────────────&gt;│ PriceCache                   │ reachable
                              └──────────────────────────────┘
 The GC starts at roots (locals, static fields) and keeps only what it
 can reach from them. The "old draft" string's memory can be reused.
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                This article shows how to check what the GC is doing before changing settings. You will compare how much memory
                two small pieces of code allocate, learn why large objects and GC settings matter, see how disposal, finalizers,
                and events affect how long objects stay alive, and see when special features such as LOH compaction or a no-GC
                region may help. You do not need prior experience tuning the GC; basic C# and .NET knowledge is enough.
            </p>

            <p>
                The article moves in three stages, in the order you should work: first measure and reduce allocations, then
                control how long objects and operating-system resources stay alive, and only then consider GC settings such as
                server mode, DATAS, LOH compaction, and no-GC regions.
            </p>
        </What>

        <Why>
            <p>
                Creating many temporary objects can make an application do extra cleanup work. For example, each change to a C#
                string creates a new string, so repeatedly building a long string can create many temporary copies.
            </p>

            <p>
                A GC collection may briefly pause your C# code while it checks objects. Large objects use a special area called
                the <Highlight>large object heap (LOH)</Highlight>. Objects that stay reachable for a long time also remain in
                memory. Meanwhile, files and network connections need to be closed by your code. These problems can all look
                like “high memory use,” but each needs a different fix.
            </p>

            <p>
                Calling <Highlight>GC.Collect()</Highlight> does not tell you what caused high memory use. It can pause your
                application, and it cannot remove objects your code can still reach. Measure first, then fix the specific cause.
            </p>

            <p>
                Some collections pause your application more than others. Generation 0 and generation 1 collections are always
                foreground (blocking) collections: .NET pauses your application threads while it checks memory, but these
                collections are usually short. Background GC applies only to generation 2 collections. It does much of its work
                on a dedicated GC thread while your application continues running, but it still needs brief pauses.
            </p>

            <p>
                One short pause rarely hurts. The cost comes from how often pauses happen: if a busy request path allocates so
                quickly that small collections start again and again, those short pauses add up to time your users can notice.
                That is why lowering the allocation rate often helps more than changing a GC setting. A collection count by
                itself does not tell you how long users waited.
            </p>
        </Why>

        <How>
            <h4 id="measure-allocation-pressure-before-tuning">Measure allocation pressure before tuning GC settings</h4>
            <p>
                Start by measuring how much memory your code creates instead of forcing a collection. This console example
                compares creating a new 1-kilobyte buffer 500 times with creating one buffer and reusing it. It runs each method
                once before measuring to avoid counting the first run as the only result. Treat the numbers as a simple example,
                not as a formal performance test.
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System;

var allocationBytes = Measure(AllocateEachTime);
var reuseBytes = Measure(ReuseBuffer);

Console.WriteLine("Strategy               Allocated bytes");
Console.WriteLine($"New buffer per item    {allocationBytes,15:N0}");
Console.WriteLine($"Reuse one buffer       {reuseBytes,15:N0}");

long Measure(Func&lt;int&gt; operation)
{
    _ = operation();
    var before = GC.GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread();
    _ = operation();
    return GC.GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread() - before;
}
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                Add the two strategies below the helper in the same <Highlight>Program.cs</Highlight> file. Both do the same
                work; only the buffer handling differs:
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
int AllocateEachTime()
{
    var total = 0;
    for (var i = 0; i &lt; 500; i++)
    {
        var buffer = new byte[1024];
        buffer[0] = (byte)i;
        total += buffer[0];
    }

    return total;
}

int ReuseBuffer()
{
    var buffer = new byte[1024];
    var total = 0;
    for (var i = 0; i &lt; 500; i++)
    {
        buffer[0] = (byte)i;
        total += buffer[0];
    }

    return total;
}
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                The console prints the number of bytes allocated by each method. On a 64-bit runtime the output looks like this:
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
Strategy               Allocated bytes
New buffer per item            524,000
Reuse one buffer                 1,048
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                Each <Highlight>byte[1024]</Highlight> takes 1,048 bytes on the heap: the 1,024 data bytes plus 24 bytes for
                the object header, type pointer, and array length. Creating 500 of them costs 500 × 1,048 = 524,000 bytes,
                while the reuse version pays for just one array. Your numbers can differ slightly between runtimes, but the gap
                is what matters.
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
 New buffer per item                  Reuse one buffer
 STACK         HEAP                   STACK         HEAP
┌────────┐    ┌───────────────┐      ┌────────┐    ┌───────────────┐
│ buffer─┼─┐  │ byte[] #1   x │      │ buffer─┼───&gt;│ byte[] (only) │
└────────┘ │  │ byte[] #2   x │      └────────┘    └───────────────┘
           │  │ ...         x │
           └─&gt;│ byte[] #500   │       1 array  =     1,048 bytes
              └───────────────┘
               500 arrays = 524,000 bytes   x = garbage for the GC
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                <Highlight>GC.GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread()</Highlight> counts bytes allocated by the current thread.
                It does not tell you how much memory is still in use, how much memory the whole process uses, or what other
                threads allocated. For careful performance comparisons, use a benchmark tool such as BenchmarkDotNet.
            </p>

            <h4 id="watch-gc-activity-in-a-running-app">Watch GC activity in a running app</h4>
            <p>
                Runtime counters show how your app behaves while it runs. Useful values include how quickly it allocates memory,
                the size of its managed heap, how often it runs a full (generation 2) collection, and how much time it spends
                doing GC work. To use these counters, install the <Highlight>dotnet-counters</Highlight> tool if needed:
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-bash">
dotnet tool install --global dotnet-counters
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                Then start monitoring your app. Replace <Highlight>1234</Highlight> with your app's process ID:
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-bash">
dotnet-counters monitor --process-id 1234 System.Runtime
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                The
                <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/dotnet-counters" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                    dotnet-counters documentation
                </a>
                explains how to find the process ID and read each counter.
            </p>

            <p>
                These counters show overall GC activity, not how long each pause lasts. To inspect pauses, collect a runtime
                trace while the app handles a representative workload. Install <Highlight>dotnet-trace</Highlight> if needed,
                then collect GC events with a built-in profile:
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-bash">
dotnet tool install --global dotnet-trace
dotnet-trace collect --process-id 1234 --profile gc-collect
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                The <Highlight>gc-collect</Highlight> profile records only GC collection events, so its overhead is very low.
                Use <Highlight>--profile gc-verbose</Highlight> when you also want sampled allocation events and can accept
                more overhead. Run <Highlight>dotnet-trace list-profiles</Highlight> to see every profile your version
                supports.
            </p>

            <p>
                Replace <Highlight>1234</Highlight> with the process ID. Open the resulting trace in PerfView or a compatible
                .NET trace viewer and inspect the GC pause events. The trace helps identify individual pauses; use a load test
                or production telemetry to understand how those pauses affect request latency.
            </p>

            <h4 id="reduce-avoidable-allocations">Reduce avoidable allocations</h4>
            <p>
                Reducing unnecessary objects can reduce GC work, but “zero allocations” is not always the right goal. Reuse a
                buffer only when doing so is safe for your code. If measurement shows a hot part of your app needs improvement,
                <Highlight>stackalloc</Highlight> and <Highlight>Span&lt;T&gt;</Highlight> can work for small, short-lived
                buffers. <Highlight>ArrayPool&lt;T&gt;</Highlight> can reuse larger arrays; return a rented array in a
                <Highlight>finally</Highlight> block so it is returned even if an error occurs.
            </p>

            <p>
                The next two examples show these ideas. <Highlight>FormatOrderCode</Highlight> builds a short text value in a
                stack buffer and allocates only the final string:
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System;

Console.WriteLine(FormatOrderCode(42)); // ORD-42

static string FormatOrderCode(int id)
{
    Span&lt;char&gt; chars = stackalloc char[16];
    "ORD-".AsSpan().CopyTo(chars);
    id.TryFormat(chars[4..], out var written);
    return new string(chars[..(4 + written)]);
}
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                <Highlight>ChecksumOfLength</Highlight> borrows a larger array from the shared pool instead of creating a new
                one on every call:
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System;
using System.Buffers;

Console.WriteLine(ChecksumOfLength(10_000)); // 1273080

static int ChecksumOfLength(int length)
{
    var buffer = ArrayPool&lt;byte&gt;.Shared.Rent(length);
    try
    {
        var data = buffer.AsSpan(0, length);
        for (var i = 0; i &lt; data.Length; i++)
        {
            data[i] = (byte)i;
        }

        var sum = 0;
        foreach (var value in data)
        {
            sum += value;
        }

        return sum;
    }
    finally
    {
        ArrayPool&lt;byte&gt;.Shared.Return(buffer);
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                <Highlight>Rent</Highlight> can return an array larger than you asked for, so slice it to the length you need.
                Once you return an array to the pool, do not keep using it. Keep <Highlight>stackalloc</Highlight> buffers small
                because stack space is limited.
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
 FormatOrderCode                ChecksumOfLength
 STACK FRAME                    STACK        HEAP (shared pool)
┌───────────────────────┐      ┌────────┐   ┌──────────────────────┐
│ chars: O R D - 4 2 ...│      │ buffer─┼──&gt;│ byte[16384]  rented  │
│ freed when the method │      └────────┘   │ byte[...]    waiting │
│ returns               │                   └──────────────────────┘
└───────────────────────┘       Return puts the array back, so the next
 HEAP: only the final "ORD-42"  call reuses it instead of allocating
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                The same idea applies to building strings. C# strings cannot be changed after creation, so repeatedly joining
                strings with <Highlight>+</Highlight> can create a new intermediate string on each iteration. For repeated
                appends, <Highlight>StringBuilder</Highlight> reuses its internal buffer and creates the final string when
                you call <Highlight>ToString</Highlight>.
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System.Text;

var parts = new[] { "GC", " ", "measures", " ", "memory" };
var builder = new StringBuilder();

foreach (var part in parts)
{
    builder.Append(part);
}

var message = builder.ToString();
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                <Highlight>StringBuilder</Highlight> still allocates its builder and final string. It is useful when you append
                repeatedly; for a small, fixed number of parts, ordinary concatenation may be clearer. Measure before changing
                code just to reduce allocations.
            </p>

            <h4 id="let-the-jit-avoid-heap-allocations">Let the JIT avoid heap allocations, but do not depend on it</h4>
            <p>
                <Highlight>Escape analysis</Highlight> is how the JIT compiler checks whether an object stays inside a method or
                is passed somewhere that may keep using it later. An object <Highlight>escapes</Highlight> when the method
                returns it, stores it in a field, or passes it to code the JIT cannot fully see. If the JIT can prove an object
                does not escape, it can put that object on the stack instead of the managed heap, so the GC never has to track it.
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
static int Largest(int a, int b, int c)
{
    int[] candidates = { a, b, c }; // never leaves this method
    var largest = candidates[0];
    foreach (var value in candidates)
    {
        largest = Math.Max(largest, value);
    }

    return largest;
}

static int[] KeepForLater(int a, int b, int c) =&gt; new[] { a, b, c }; // escapes: the caller receives it
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                Starting with .NET 10, the JIT can stack-allocate small, fixed-size arrays such as
                <Highlight>candidates</Highlight> when it proves they do not escape. The array returned by
                <Highlight>KeepForLater</Highlight> must live on the heap because the caller can keep using it.
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
 Largest (optimized, .NET 10)     KeepForLater
 STACK FRAME                      STACK (caller)      HEAP
┌──────────────────────────┐     ┌──────────────┐    ┌──────────┐
│ candidates: [ a, b, c ]  │     │ result ──────┼───&gt;│ int[3]   │
│ largest                  │     └──────────────┘    └──────────┘
└──────────────────────────┘      The array outlives the method,
 Gone when the method returns,    so it must live on the heap.
 so the GC never sees it.
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                Treat this as a bonus, not a design tool. It applies only to optimized code, so a method's first runs and Debug
                builds still allocate on the heap. Large arrays, such as <Highlight>new byte[1024]</Highlight> in a loop, are
                still heap allocations. When a small buffer must stay off the heap, use <Highlight>stackalloc</Highlight>
                explicitly.
            </p>

            <p>
                A lambda that uses a local variable from outside the lambda is called a <Highlight>capturing lambda</Highlight>.
                The compiler moves the captured variable into a hidden helper object (often called a closure) so the lambda can
                keep using it, and creating that helper is an allocation. A lambda that captures nothing does not need this
                helper, and the compiler can cache its delegate and reuse it. Adding the <Highlight>static</Highlight> keyword
                does not create that caching; it makes the compiler reject any accidental capture.
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
int offset = 10;

Func&lt;int, int&gt; addOffset = value =&gt; value + offset;
Func&lt;int, int&gt; doubleValue = static value =&gt; value * 2;
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                <Highlight>addOffset</Highlight> captures <Highlight>offset</Highlight>; the static lambda cannot capture it.
                This difference matters when code creates delegates repeatedly in a performance-sensitive loop. In .NET 10 the
                JIT can sometimes stack-allocate a delegate that does not escape, but the closure object is still a heap
                allocation. Keep the delegate or move the work out of the loop when that fits the design, then check allocations
                with a profiler.
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
 STACK              HEAP
┌───────────────┐  ┌────────────────────┐  ┌──────────────────┐
│ addOffset ────┼─&gt;│ Func&lt;int, int&gt;     │─&gt;│ closure object   │
│               │  │ (new delegate)     │  │   offset = 10    │
│ doubleValue ──┼┐ └────────────────────┘  └──────────────────┘
└───────────────┘│ ┌────────────────────┐
                 └&gt;│ cached delegate    │  created once, reused,
                   │ (no captured data) │  no closure needed
                   └────────────────────┘
            </CodeSnippet>

            <h4 id="separate-managed-objects-from-operating-system-resources">Separate managed objects from operating-system resources</h4>
            <p>
                A <Highlight>managed object</Highlight> is an object whose memory is tracked by the .NET runtime, such as a
                string, list, or <Highlight>FileStream</Highlight>. The stream object is managed, but the open file handle it
                uses belongs to the operating system. The GC can reclaim the stream object's memory when your code can no longer
                reach it. The handle may then be released later through finalization, but the timing is unpredictable; use
                <Highlight>Dispose</Highlight> when you need to close the file promptly.
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
 STACK        MANAGED HEAP (tracked by the GC)        OPERATING SYSTEM
┌────────┐   ┌────────────┐   ┌────────────────┐     ┌─────────────┐
│ stream─┼──&gt;│ FileStream │──&gt;│ SafeFileHandle │────&gt;│ file handle │
└────────┘   └────────────┘   └────────────────┘     └─────────────┘
              The GC reclaims these objects'          Dispose closes
              memory when they are unreachable        this right away
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                <Highlight>IDisposable</Highlight> lets an object expose a <Highlight>Dispose</Highlight> method so your code
                can release resources at a predictable time. Calling <Highlight>Dispose</Highlight> does not tell the GC to
                collect the object or remove it from memory. It releases resources the object owns; the GC reclaims the object's
                memory later, when your code can no longer reach it.
            </p>

            <p>
                Use a <Highlight>using</Highlight> statement for disposable objects. C# calls
                <Highlight>Dispose</Highlight> when execution leaves the block, including when an exception occurs.
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System.IO;

using (var stream = File.OpenRead("report.json"))
{
    // Read the file here.
}

// Dispose has run here, so the file handle can be released.
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                The compiler turns a <Highlight>using</Highlight> block into a <Highlight>try/finally</Highlight> block that
                calls <Highlight>Dispose</Highlight> in <Highlight>finally</Highlight>. You can also write a using
                declaration, such as <Highlight>using var stream = File.OpenRead("report.json");</Highlight>, which
                disposes the object at the end of the enclosing scope.
            </p>

            <p>
                Clearing lists or setting fields to <Highlight>null</Highlight> inside <Highlight>Dispose</Highlight> is
                usually unnecessary. Once nothing references the disposed object, the GC can reclaim everything it points to.
                Use <Highlight>Dispose</Highlight> for things the GC cannot handle: closing handles and connections, and
                releasing native memory.
            </p>

            <p>
                If your class owns another object that implements <Highlight>IDisposable</Highlight>, dispose that object when
                your own class is disposed. Only dispose resources your class owns; do not dispose an object that was merely
                passed in for temporary use unless the ownership contract says you took responsibility for it.
            </p>

            <h4 id="implement-disposal-for-owned-resources">Implement disposal for resources your class owns</h4>
            <p>
                A sealed class can often implement <Highlight>Dispose</Highlight> directly. A class designed for inheritance
                can use a protected virtual <Highlight>Dispose(bool disposing)</Highlight> method, which gives derived classes
                a place to release the resources they add. Dispose managed child objects only when
                <Highlight>disposing</Highlight> is true; a finalizer must not rely on other managed objects still being
                available.
            </p>

            <p>
                A class that only owns other disposable objects, such as streams or database connections, does not need a
                finalizer. Those objects already handle their own unmanaged cleanup. A finalizer is needed only when your class
                holds an unmanaged resource directly, which is the case in the example below.
            </p>

            <p>
                <Highlight>GC.SuppressFinalize</Highlight> tells the runtime not to run this object's finalizer after explicit
                disposal; it does not trigger collection or free the object's managed memory. It matters only if this object
                or a derived type has a finalizer. Calling it also affects performance. When the GC finds an unreachable
                object that still needs finalization, it cannot free that object yet. It queues the object for the finalizer
                thread and keeps it, and everything it references, alive at least until a later collection. That often promotes
                the object to an older generation. After <Highlight>SuppressFinalize</Highlight>, the object can be reclaimed
                like any other object.
            </p>

            <p>
                If a class directly owns unmanaged memory, the finalizer is its safety net when a caller forgets to call
                <Highlight>Dispose</Highlight>. Cleanup can start in two ways: <Highlight>Dispose()</Highlight> calls
                <Highlight>Dispose(true)</Highlight>, while the finalizer calls <Highlight>Dispose(false)</Highlight>. Release
                unmanaged resources in both cases, and touch other managed objects only when <Highlight>disposing</Highlight>
                is true.
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
Your code calls Dispose()               GC finds the object unreachable
           │                                        │
           v                                        v
Dispose(disposing: true)                Finalizer runs Dispose(disposing: false)
  ├─ dispose owned managed objects        ├─ skip managed objects (they may
  ├─ free unmanaged resources             │   already be finalized)
  └─ GC.SuppressFinalize(this)            └─ free unmanaged resources
           │                                        │
           v                                        v
Reclaimed by a normal collection        Reclaimed by a later collection
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                Both paths end in one shared method, so the cleanup logic lives in one place. The class exposes the two entry
                points like this:
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;

public class NativeScratchBuffer : IDisposable
{
    private IntPtr _memory;
    private bool _disposed;

    public NativeScratchBuffer(int sizeInBytes)
    {
        _memory = Marshal.AllocHGlobal(sizeInBytes);
    }

    ~NativeScratchBuffer() =&gt; Dispose(disposing: false);

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Dispose(disposing: true);
        GC.SuppressFinalize(this);
    }

    // Dispose(bool) is shown next.
}
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                Inside the same class, the shared method checks the guard, cleans up managed objects only on the
                <Highlight>Dispose()</Highlight> path, and always frees the native memory:
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
protected virtual void Dispose(bool disposing)
{
    if (_disposed)
    {
        return;
    }

    if (disposing)
    {
        // Dispose managed objects this class owns.
    }

    if (_memory != IntPtr.Zero)
    {
        Marshal.FreeHGlobal(_memory);
        _memory = IntPtr.Zero;
    }

    _disposed = true;
}
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                This example shows how the two paths fit together. In real code, prefer <Highlight>SafeHandle</Highlight> to
                wrap a low-level operating-system handle, because it already provides reliable cleanup if normal disposal is
                missed. Add a custom finalizer only when your type directly owns an unmanaged resource and cannot use
                <Highlight>SafeHandle</Highlight>.
            </p>

            <p>
                The <Highlight>protected virtual</Highlight> method is what makes the pattern work with inheritance. A derived
                class does not implement <Highlight>IDisposable</Highlight> again or write its own finalizer. It overrides
                <Highlight>Dispose(bool)</Highlight>, releases what it added, and then hands control back to the base class:
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System.IO;

public sealed class TracedScratchBuffer : NativeScratchBuffer
{
    private readonly StreamWriter _log;
    private bool _disposed;

    public TracedScratchBuffer(int sizeInBytes, string logPath)
        : base(sizeInBytes)
    {
        _log = new StreamWriter(logPath);
    }

    protected override void Dispose(bool disposing)
    {
        if (!_disposed)
        {
            if (disposing)
            {
                _log.Dispose();
            }

            _disposed = true;
        }

        base.Dispose(disposing);
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                Calling <Highlight>Dispose()</Highlight> on a <Highlight>TracedScratchBuffer</Highlight> runs the base
                class's public method, which calls the override. The override closes the log, and
                <Highlight>base.Dispose(disposing)</Highlight> frees the native memory. Callers can dispose the whole object
                with one call, without knowing what each class in the hierarchy owns. Both classes guard against repeated
                calls, so disposing twice is harmless.
            </p>

            <h4 id="unsubscribe-events-and-stop-timers">Unsubscribe events and stop timers</h4>
            <p>
                Events can also keep objects alive. When one object subscribes to an event on another object, the publisher keeps
                a reference to the subscriber until it unsubscribes. Unsubscribe when you no longer need the event; dropping
                your own reference to the subscriber does not remove the publisher's reference. A good place to unsubscribe is
                the subscriber's <Highlight>Dispose</Highlight> method.
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System;

public sealed class StockTicker
{
    public event EventHandler&lt;decimal&gt;? PriceChanged;

    public void Publish(decimal price) =&gt; PriceChanged?.Invoke(this, price);
}

public sealed class PriceBadge : IDisposable
{
    private readonly StockTicker _ticker;

    public PriceBadge(StockTicker ticker)
    {
        _ticker = ticker;
        _ticker.PriceChanged += OnPriceChanged;
    }

    public void Dispose() =&gt; _ticker.PriceChanged -= OnPriceChanged;

    private void OnPriceChanged(object? sender, decimal price) =&gt;
        Console.WriteLine($"Badge shows {price}");
}
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                If a long-lived <Highlight>StockTicker</Highlight> creates and drops thousands of badges without disposing them,
                each badge stays alive in the event's handler list. That is a memory leak, even though your code no longer
                uses those badges.
            </p>

            <p>
                Timers behave in a similar way. A started <Highlight>System.Timers.Timer</Highlight> is kept alive by the
                runtime's timer infrastructure, so it keeps raising <Highlight>Elapsed</Highlight> after your code loses
                every reference to it. Its handler also keeps the handler's target object alive. When the owner is done, stop
                the timer, unsubscribe from <Highlight>Elapsed</Highlight>, and dispose the timer, usually from the owner's
                <Highlight>Dispose</Highlight> method.
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
 LONG-LIVED ROOT         HEAP
 static ticker ────────&gt; StockTicker
                          └─ PriceChanged handler list
                               ├─&gt; delegate ─&gt; PriceBadge #1
                               ├─&gt; delegate ─&gt; PriceBadge #2
                               └─&gt; ...
 Your code dropped every badge, but the handler list still reaches them.
 PriceBadge.Dispose() removes its delegate, so the badge becomes garbage.

 Timer:  runtime timer queue ─&gt; Timer ─&gt; Elapsed delegate ─&gt; owner object
            </CodeSnippet>

            <h4 id="use-weak-references-only-for-optional-data">Use weak references only for optional data</h4>
            <p>
                A <Highlight>WeakReference&lt;T&gt;</Highlight> lets you refer to an object without keeping it alive. The GC
                may collect the target whenever no strong references remain. This can suit an optional cache: use the cached
                object if it is still available, or recreate it if the GC has collected it.
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
public sealed class OptionalBufferCache
{
    private WeakReference&lt;byte[]&gt;? _cachedBuffer;

    public byte[] GetBuffer()
    {
        if (_cachedBuffer is not null
            &amp;&amp; _cachedBuffer.TryGetTarget(out var existingBuffer))
        {
            return existingBuffer;
        }

        var buffer = new byte[1024];
        _cachedBuffer = new WeakReference&lt;byte[]&gt;(buffer);
        return buffer;
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
 HEAP
 OptionalBufferCache ─&gt; WeakReference&lt;byte[]&gt; ---&gt; byte[1024]
                        (weak: the GC ignores it)      ^
 STACK                                                 │ strong
 caller's local variable ──────────────────────────────┘
 While the caller holds the array, it stays alive. Once only the weak
 reference is left, the GC may collect it and TryGetTarget returns false.
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                The returned array is strongly referenced by the caller, but the cache does not keep it alive by itself. If
                nothing else needs the array, the GC may collect it and the next call creates another one. Do not use a weak
                reference as a substitute for stopping a timer, disposing a resource, or unsubscribing an event; use explicit
                ownership and cleanup for those jobs. For a deeper introduction to reachability, continue with
                <a href="/blogs/understanding-dotnet-garbage-collection-generations-scope-and-object-lifetimes">
                    Understanding .NET Garbage Collection: Generations, Scope, and Object Lifetimes
                </a>.
            </p>

            <h4 id="select-workstation-or-server-gc-for-the-workload">Select workstation or server GC for the workload</h4>
            <p>
                .NET has two main GC modes: workstation and server. They make different trade-offs:
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <Highlight>Workstation GC</Highlight> is designed for apps running on a user's computer, where the app shares
                    the machine with many other programs. It uses smaller allocation budgets, so it collects more often and keeps
                    memory use lower.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <Highlight>Server GC</Highlight> treats the app as the main workload on the machine. By default, it creates
                    one heap and one dedicated GC thread per logical CPU and collects those heaps in parallel. It allows more
                    allocation between collections, so it usually runs fewer collections, completes more work, and uses more
                    memory.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
 Workstation GC              Server GC on a 4-CPU machine
┌────────────────────┐      ┌────────┐┌────────┐┌────────┐┌────────┐
│ one managed heap   │      │ heap 0 ││ heap 1 ││ heap 2 ││ heap 3 │
└────────────────────┘      └────────┘└────────┘└────────┘└────────┘
 smaller budgets,             one GC thread per heap, collected in
 more frequent GCs            parallel; DATAS starts at 1 heap and grows
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                ASP.NET Core apps use server GC by default. Console, desktop, and worker apps use workstation GC unless you change
                it. You can set the mode in the project file:
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-xml">
&lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
  &lt;ServerGarbageCollection&gt;true&lt;/ServerGarbageCollection&gt;
&lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                You can also set it without rebuilding, through <Highlight>System.GC.Server</Highlight> in
                <Highlight>runtimeconfig.json</Highlight> or the <Highlight>DOTNET_gcServer</Highlight> environment variable.
            </p>

            <p>
                Neither mode is always faster. Test both with the kind of work your app does in production. Compare how much work
                it completes, how long pauses last, and how much memory it uses. A server running many copies of an app may need
                different settings from a server running one busy app.
            </p>

            <h4 id="let-datas-adapt-server-gc-to-real-memory-needs">Let DATAS adapt server GC to real memory needs</h4>
            <p>
                Plain server GC sizes itself mainly for throughput and the hardware it sees. That causes two problems. The same app
                can use very different amounts of memory on a small machine and a large one. And after a traffic spike ends, the
                heap may not shrink much, which matters when many containers share one node.
            </p>

            <p>
                <Highlight>Dynamic Adaptation to Application Sizes (DATAS)</Highlight> addresses this. It aims to keep the heap
                roughly proportional to the data your app keeps alive for a long time. It starts with a single heap and adds or
                removes heaps as the load changes, so it can act anywhere between workstation-like and full server-like
                behavior. DATAS was introduced as an opt-in feature in .NET 8 and is enabled by default for server GC starting
                in .NET 9. It does not apply to workstation GC.
            </p>

            <p>
                The trade-off is more frequent generation 0 and 1 collections and a small throughput cost, in exchange for a much
                smaller working set, especially for bursty workloads and memory-limited containers. If measurements show that
                your app needs that throughput back, you can turn DATAS off:
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-xml">
&lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
  &lt;ServerGarbageCollection&gt;true&lt;/ServerGarbageCollection&gt;
  &lt;GarbageCollectionAdaptationMode&gt;0&lt;/GarbageCollectionAdaptationMode&gt;
&lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                The <Highlight>DOTNET_GCDynamicAdaptationMode</Highlight> environment variable does the same job, where
                <Highlight>1</Highlight> enables DATAS and <Highlight>0</Highlight> disables it. Setting a fixed heap count
                with <Highlight>GCHeapCount</Highlight> stops the GC from growing and shrinking the number of heaps, which is
                a core part of how DATAS adapts. Treat these two settings as alternatives, and change either one only after
                measuring.
            </p>

            <p>
                DATAS is not a hard cap on memory. When an app runs in a container with a memory limit, .NET treats that limit as
                the machine's memory, and by default the GC heap hard limit is 75% of it. That hard limit bounds the GC heap's
                committed memory, including GC bookkeeping, but not total process memory. A container limit applies to memory
                charged to the container, which can include multiple processes and native memory. That budget can be exhausted
                and cause an out-of-memory kill without a managed <Highlight>OutOfMemoryException</Highlight>. See the
                <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/runtime-config/garbage-collector" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                    GC runtime configuration reference
                </a>
                and the
                <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/garbage-collection/datas" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                    DATAS documentation
                </a>
                before configuring either limit.
            </p>

            <h4 id="treat-loh-compaction-as-a-measured-maintenance-action">Treat LOH compaction as a measured maintenance action</h4>
            <p>
                Objects that are 85,000 bytes or larger go on the LOH and are collected with generation 2 objects. You can check
                this with <Highlight>GC.GetGeneration</Highlight>, which reports the generation an object currently belongs to:
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
var smallArray = new byte[1_024];
var largeArray = new byte[100_000];

Console.WriteLine($"Small array generation: {GC.GetGeneration(smallArray)}");
Console.WriteLine($"Large array generation: {GC.GetGeneration(largeArray)}");
            </CodeSnippet>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
Small array generation: 0
Large array generation: 2
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                The large array reports generation 2 as soon as it is created, because the LOH is collected only during full
                collections. <Highlight>GC.CollectionCount(2)</Highlight> and <Highlight>GC.GetTotalMemory(false)</Highlight>
                are also useful for quick experiments. In optimized code, the JIT may treat a local variable as unused after
                its last read, so the GC can collect the object before the end of the method. If an experiment needs an object
                to stay alive until a certain line, call <Highlight>GC.KeepAlive(obj)</Highlight> there.
            </p>

            <p>
                By default, .NET usually leaves gaps where unused large objects were instead of moving the remaining objects to
                fill those gaps. This avoids the cost of copying large blocks of memory. A large LOH does not automatically mean
                there is a problem; .NET may reuse those gaps for future allocations.
            </p>

            <p>
                If profiling shows that gaps in the LOH are causing a problem, you can ask .NET to compact it during the next
                full, blocking collection. Compaction moves objects to close gaps and can pause your app. This is a one-time
                request, not a general memory cleanup switch. After that collection compacts the LOH, the setting returns to
                <Highlight>Default</Highlight> automatically.
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
 SMALL OBJECT HEAP              LARGE OBJECT HEAP (85,000 bytes or more)
┌───────┬───────┬───────┐      ┌────────┬──────┬────────┬──────┐
│ gen 0 │ gen 1 │ gen 2 │      │ 100 KB │ free │ 300 KB │ free │
└───────┴───────┴───────┘      └────────┴──────┴────────┴──────┘
 survivors are usually          collected with gen 2; gaps are reused
 compacted (moved together)     for new large objects, not closed

                                After CompactOnce + a full blocking GC:
                               ┌────────┬────────┬─────────────┐
                               │ 100 KB │ 300 KB │ free space  │
                               └────────┴────────┴─────────────┘
            </CodeSnippet>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System.Runtime;

GCSettings.LargeObjectHeapCompactionMode =
    GCLargeObjectHeapCompactionMode.CompactOnce;

// Optional: compact now instead of waiting for the next full blocking GC.
GC.Collect();

Console.WriteLine(GCSettings.LargeObjectHeapCompactionMode); // Default
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                Usually it is enough to set the mode and let the next full blocking collection do the work. Force it with
                <Highlight>GC.Collect()</Highlight> only at a controlled moment, such as after a large batch job finishes, and
                never as a routine step. First confirm that LOH gaps are the problem, then test the change with a workload
                similar to your real app.
            </p>

            <h4 id="reserve-no-gc-regions-only-for-bounded-work">Reserve no-GC regions only for bounded work</h4>
            <p>
                A <Highlight>no-GC region</Highlight> is a short period when .NET tries not to start a garbage collection.
                <Highlight>GC.TryStartNoGCRegion</Highlight> asks .NET to start one and reserve a memory budget for it. The
                request can fail if .NET cannot reserve enough memory, and it may run a collection first to make room. If it
                succeeds, keep allocations within the budget and call <Highlight>GC.EndNoGCRegion()</Highlight> when the work
                is finished.
            </p>

            <p>
                If your app uses more memory than the reserved budget, .NET ends the region early. A collection can run, and
                <Highlight>GCSettings.LatencyMode</Highlight> returns to its previous value. Calling
                <Highlight>GC.EndNoGCRegion()</Highlight> after that throws <Highlight>InvalidOperationException</Highlight>,
                so check the latency mode first:
            </p>

            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System;
using System.Runtime;

const long budgetBytes = 32 * 1024 * 1024;

if (GC.TryStartNoGCRegion(budgetBytes))
{
    try
    {
        ProcessAudioBlock();
    }
    finally
    {
        if (GCSettings.LatencyMode == GCLatencyMode.NoGCRegion)
        {
            GC.EndNoGCRegion();
        }
    }
}
else
{
    ProcessAudioBlock();
}

static void ProcessAudioBlock()
{
    // Short, bounded work that allocates far less than the budget.
}
            </CodeSnippet>

            <p>
                The budget has an upper limit that depends on the runtime and GC mode, and asking for too much throws
                <Highlight>ArgumentOutOfRangeException</Highlight>. Use no-GC regions only for short, measured work where a
                pause would cause a real problem, such as a real-time audio block or a latency-critical calculation, and not for
                an ordinary request. In most apps, reducing unnecessary allocations is a simpler and safer choice.
            </p>

            <p>
                For deeper runtime behavior, use the official
                <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/garbage-collection/large-object-heap" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                    large object heap documentation
                </a>,
                <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/garbage-collection/workstation-server-gc" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                    workstation and server GC documentation
                </a>, and
                <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/runtime-config/garbage-collector" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                    garbage collector runtime configuration reference
                </a>.
            </p>
        </How>

        <Summary>
            <p>
                Work in the same order as this article: measure first, fix allocations and lifetimes next, and change GC settings
                last.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>Measure allocation rates and runtime pauses before changing GC settings; frequent short pauses add up.</li>
                <li>Cut avoidable allocations with buffer reuse, <Highlight>StringBuilder</Highlight>, <Highlight>Span&lt;T&gt;</Highlight>, <Highlight>stackalloc</Highlight>, and <Highlight>ArrayPool&lt;T&gt;</Highlight>; treat .NET 10 stack allocation as a bonus, not a guarantee.</li>
                <li>Dispose the resources your class owns. In the dispose pattern, call <Highlight>GC.SuppressFinalize</Highlight> from <Highlight>Dispose()</Highlight> so finalizable objects are not kept alive for an extra collection.</li>
                <li>Unsubscribe events and stop timers explicitly; publishers and running timers keep objects alive even after your code drops its references.</li>
                <li>Use <Highlight>WeakReference&lt;T&gt;</Highlight> only for optional data you can re-create, never as a replacement for cleanup.</li>
                <li>Choose workstation or server GC based on production measurements; with server GC, keep DATAS on unless measurements show you need the throughput back.</li>
                <li>Use the LOH compaction request only when diagnostics show fragmentation and a full blocking collection is acceptable.</li>
                <li>Keep no-GC regions short, bounded, and exceptional; they are not a general GC optimization.</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                Need a refresher on the basics? The earlier articles in this series cover
                <a href="/blogs/garbage-collection-fundamentals-in-dotnet">
                    Garbage Collection Fundamentals in .NET
                </a>
                and
                <a href="/blogs/understanding-dotnet-garbage-collection-generations-scope-and-object-lifetimes">
                    generations, scope, and object lifetimes
                </a>.
            </p>
        </Summary>
